For every $X,G,D,\ell$ as above, the natural addition maps $$\label{intro:decomposition}
 \bigoplus_{\mathcal O}C_{D,\mathcal O,\mathbb Q}
       \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }C_{D,\mathbb Q},
 \qquad
 \bigoplus_{\mathcal O}C_{D,\mathcal O,\ell}
       \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }C_{D,\ell}$$ are isomorphisms.

At a hyperspecial closed-place leg of degree $d$, the functor $\mathsf K$ on perfect spherical labels has the form $$\label{cat:loop-pairing}
 \mathsf K(P)\simeq
 \left(\mathcal A\otimes_{B_{q^d}}
                  (B_{q^d}\otimes_{S^{\otimes d}}P)\right)^{\widehat G},$$ where $\mathcal A$ is an equivariant dg module, with no coherence or boundedness assumption.

For every such $L$ and every fixed finite-dimensional rational $H_L$-representation $U$, there is a constant $C(U,L)$ such that $$\label{amp:levi-bound}
 H^i\bigl((M_L\otimes U\otimes\eta)^{H_L}\bigr)=0
 \quad\text{for }i>C(U,L),\qquad \eta\in X^*(L).$$

For every effective divisor $D$, the rational cuspidal space has the decomposition $$\label{glob:rational-decomposition}
             C_{D,\mathbb Q}
                 =\bigoplus_{\mathcal O}C_{D,\mathcal O,\mathbb Q}$$ with the spectral-support summands specified in the introduction.

outputs.exact_statement:
For every $X,G,D,\ell$ as above, the natural addition maps $$\label{intro:decomposition}
 \bigoplus_{\mathcal O}C_{D,\mathcal O,\mathbb Q}
       \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }C_{D,\mathbb Q},
 \qquad
 \bigoplus_{\mathcal O}C_{D,\mathcal O,\ell}
       \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }C_{D,\ell}$$ are isomorphisms. The sums range over the nilpotent orbits of $\widehat G$.

The preceding construction is an exact enhanced Frobenius-twisted cyclic functor. Put $\Phi=\mathrm{Fr}^*$ on local kernels. With convolution ordered in the direction of the path, its cyclic maps are $$\label{cat:last-first}
 \rho_{U,V}:\mathsf K(U\star V)
       \xrightarrow{\sim}\mathsf K(\Phi(V)\star U).$$ They are natural in all enhanced morphisms and coherently compatible with units, iterated products, and change of level. Here $\rho_{U,\mathbf1}$ is the identity. The full slide $\rho_{\mathbf1,V}:\mathsf K(V)\to\mathsf K(\Phi V)$, followed by the Weil structure of $V$, is cohomological Frobenius. Slides at disjoint sets of legs commute; their product is the full slide.

At a hyperspecial closed-place leg of degree $d$, the functor $\mathsf K$ on perfect spherical labels has the form $$\label{cat:loop-pairing}
 \mathsf K(P)\simeq
 \left(\mathcal A\otimes_{B_{q^d}}
                  (B_{q^d}\otimes_{S^{\otimes d}}P)\right)^{\widehat G},$$ where $\mathcal A$ is an equivariant dg module, with no coherence or boundedness assumption. After eliminating intermediate factors, the algebra is $$\label{cat:loop-algebra}
 B_r=\mathcal O(\widehat G)\otimes
       \operatorname{Sym}(\widehat{\mathfrak g}^*[-2]\oplus\widehat{\mathfrak g}^*[-1]),
 \qquad r=q^d.$$ Write $s$ for its degree-zero holonomy variable and $e$ for its degree-two linear variable. The second summand supplies odd Koszul generators $\eta_\alpha$ of degree $1$, with $$\label{cat:holonomy}
 d\eta_\alpha=
       \langle\alpha,\operatorname{Ad}(s)e-q^d e\rangle.$$ The group acts by simultaneous conjugation.*

*The $d$-fold selected-leg slide on representation labels is the action of $s$ on each factor. Spherical Hecke characters act by the corresponding invariant characters of $s$. On cohomology these descriptions agree with the transfers in Proposition <a href="#cat:hecke" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="cat:hecke">8</a>. A degree-two morphism linear in the selected $e$ variable multiplies the corresponding slide eigenvalue by $q^d$. All statements are natural in perfect labels and their dg morphisms.

For every such $L$ and every fixed finite-dimensional rational $H_L$-representation $U$, there is a constant $C(U,L)$ such that $$\label{amp:levi-bound}
 H^i\bigl((M_L\otimes U\otimes\eta)^{H_L}\bigr)=0
 \quad\text{for }i>C(U,L),\qquad \eta\in X^*(L).$$ Invariants denote invariant global sections. The constant is independent of the character $\eta$.

In the finite Koszul specialization at $[t]$ of $\mathsf K(V_\mu^{\boxtimes d})$, retain the part with maximal absolute value for the $d$-fold slide at the chosen leg. Its cohomological upper bound is $$\label{amp:amplitude-bound}
 C+\max_{e\in E_t}d\langle\mu,h_e\rangle.$$ The constant is independent of $m$. It is also uniform when $t$ is replaced by $tz$, with $z\in Z(\widehat G)^\circ$, after omitting the absolute-value central part, and when one chooses among a fixed finite list of hyperspecial variants. Fixed additional kernels at disjoint places are permitted.*

*Unless $t$, modulo its absolute-value central part, is a compact factor times a Jacobson–Morozov point for $Q$, the difference between $2d\langle\lambda,\mu\rangle$ and the nonconstant term in Equation <a href="#amp:amplitude-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="amp:amplitude-bound">[amp:amplitude-bound]</a> is positive and grows linearly with $m$.

Let $u$ satisfy the chamber, divisibility, degree-zero, and genericity conditions above. Let $v$ be any fixed regular positive tuple in $\Lambda_0^r$, without a parity restriction. For all sufficiently large integers $n$, $$\label{rot:normalized-trace}
 \sum_{[\mathcal E]}(T_{nu+v})_{\mathcal E,\mathcal E}
   =\operatorname{Tr}_{\mathrm{alt}}
           (\mathrm{Fr}^{n}T_v\mid M(u)).$$ The diagonal sum uses ordinary matrix entries on the rational isomorphism classes of bundles with level, and is finite. The assertion retains arbitrary multiplicities in $D$ and the allowed central quotient.

For sufficiently large $n$, the fixed-point groupoid for $\mathrm{Fr}^{n*}U_v$ on $Y_u$ is equivalent to $\mathcal L_{nu+v}^{\mathrm{sh}}$. Consequently $$\label{rot:shifted-trace}
 \operatorname{Tr}_{\mathrm{alt}}(\mathrm{Fr}^{n*}U_v\mid C(u))
                  =|\mathcal L_{nu+v}^{\mathrm{sh}}|.$$

Let $G$ be split connected reductive, let $x$ be a closed point, and impose Iwahori level at $x$ and arbitrary fixed compact open level away from $x$. Let $\pi$ be a unitary discrete automorphic representation with nonzero invariants at that level. If an ordered Bernstein weight $t_0$ of $\pi_x$ has dominant exponent $\lambda_0=\log_{q_x}|t_0|$, then $$\label{ext:local-form}
 t_0\sim
 \phi\begin{pmatrix}q_x^{1/2}&0\\0&q_x^{-1/2}\end{pmatrix}c,$$ where $\phi\colon\operatorname{SL}_2\to\widehat G$ is a homomorphism and $c$ is semisimple and conjugate into a maximal compact subgroup of $Z_{\widehat G}(\phi)$.

At a fixed compact open level the following assertions hold.*

1.  *The discrete summand $\mathcal D_K$ of $\mathcal L_K$ is finite-dimensional. Its orthogonal complement admits a Hecke-equivariant unitary realization as a closed invariant subspace of a finite sum of direct integrals of normalized parabolic inductions $$\operatorname{Ind}_{P(\mathbb A_F)}^{G(\mathbb A_F)}
                     (\sigma\otimes\chi),$$ where $P$ is proper, $\sigma$ belongs to the discrete spectrum of its Levi $M$, and $\chi$ ranges over a compact torus of unitary unramified characters, subject to the central condition imposed by $\Xi$. All fixed-level fiber dimensions are bounded.*


2.  *Let $Q_b$ be the orthogonal projection onto the point basis in an HN ball of radius $b$. There are constants $C,c>0$ such that $$\label{ext:discrete-tail}
       \|(1-Q_b)v\|\le C e^{-cb}\|v\|
           \qquad(v\in\mathcal D_K, b\ge0).$$*


3.  *Suppose that the level at $x$ is Iwahori. The ordered Bernstein weights of a fiber induced from $M$ have the form $$\label{ext:induced-weights}
          w(t_M z),\qquad w\Phi_M^+\subset\Phi_G^+,$$ where $t_M$ is an ordered Bernstein weight of $\sigma_x$ and $z$ is the local value of $\chi$. There are finitely many such weight families, and their generalized-weight lengths are uniformly bounded.

Under the induction assumption and the choice of a violating weight above, let $u=(\mu,\zeta)$, where $\mu$ is sufficiently large in the cone of Lemma <a href="#ext:spectral-gap" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="ext:spectral-gap">30</a> and $u$ satisfies Equation <a href="#rot:genericity" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="rot:genericity">[rot:genericity]</a>. Then $M(u)$ contains the joint Bernstein character $(t_0,t_{0,y})$ on $(L^0)^2$. At some full ordered extension $(t_x,t_y)$ of that character it has nonzero cohomology in a degree $i$ satisfying $$\label{ext:degree-lower}
    i\ge 2d_x\langle\lambda_0,\mu\rangle
                       +2\log_q|\zeta(t_{0,y})|.$$

The algebra $\mathbb T_{D,\mathbb Q}$ is finite dimensional and reduced. After extending scalars to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, its action has a decomposition $$\label{glob:joint-spectrum}
 \overline{\mathbb Q}\otimes_{\mathbb Q}C_{D,\mathbb Q}
   =\bigoplus_{\chi\in\Sigma_D} C_\chi$$ into joint eigenspaces, where $\Sigma_D$ is finite. All good-place Satake classes of these characters are algebraic.

Let $\mathcal V$ be a lisse $\overline{\mathbb Q}_\ell$-sheaf on the smooth geometrically connected curve $U/\mathbb F_q$, defined over a finite extension of $\mathbb Q_\ell$. Suppose that all its Frobenius eigenvalues at closed points are algebraic over $\mathbb Q$. For each complex embedding of these algebraic numbers, the multiset of numbers $$\log_{q_x}|\alpha|,
       \qquad \alpha\text{ an eigenvalue of }
                        \operatorname{Frob}_x\text{ on }\mathcal V,$$ counted with multiplicity, is independent of $x\in|U|$.

For every effective divisor $D$, the rational cuspidal space has the decomposition $$\label{glob:rational-decomposition}
             C_{D,\mathbb Q}
                 =\bigoplus_{\mathcal O}C_{D,\mathcal O,\mathbb Q}$$ with the spectral-support summands specified in the introduction.
